## Reference kernels hoisted into callable MATLAB functions.
##
## `Lines` addresses the UNMODIFIED reference script. tools/extract.R copies
## that range byte for byte between VERBATIM markers and adds nothing but a
## signature, comments and `end`; tools/check_extraction re-reads the source and
## fails if a generated file drifted by a byte.
##
## `ConsumedOnly: v=i` asserts the slice touches input `v` at index `i` and
## nowhere else, which is what makes it safe to supply a stub for it on a
## dataset that has no such variable.
##
## Generated functions are NOT committed -- they reproduce code from a
## repository this project does not vendor (matlab_reference/ is gitignored as
## re-clonable). Run tools/run_parity.R to regenerate.

Function: ref_filter_scalar
Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Lines: 131-172
Inputs: x, y, L_x, f_x, f_y, L_y, Sx_1, Sx_2, Sy_1, Sy_2, N, dt
Outputs: filter_mean, filter_cov, S_xoS_x, S_xoS_x_inv, S_yoS_y, S_yoS_x,
 S_xoS_y
ConsumedOnly: y=1
Pairs: aci_filter
Note: The forward CGNS filter, together with the four noise Grammians it
 derives on the way in. Two initial conditions are literals inside this range
 rather than arguments: the mean starts at y(1), the true latent value, and
 the covariance at a hardcoded 0.1. The mean is reachable -- `y` is consumed
 only at index 1, asserted above, so a one-element stub selects it -- but the
 covariance is not. R0 is therefore pinned at 0.1 for every paired comparison,
 and aciR's R0 argument is an interface extension, not a matched parameter.

Function: ref_smoother_scalar
Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Lines: 178-241
Inputs: x, filter_mean, filter_cov, L_x, f_x, f_y, L_y, S_xoS_x_inv, S_yoS_y,
 S_yoS_x, S_xoS_y, N, dt
Outputs: smoother_mean, smoother_cov, E_j_matrices, F_j_matrices
Pairs: aci_smoother, .aci_online_aux
Note: One reference range, two aciR functions. The backward smoother and the
 online smoother's per-step auxiliary matrices E_j and F_j are computed in the
 same loop here; aciR factors the auxiliaries into .aci_online_aux() because
 the online smoother needs them without the backward pass. The pairing is
 therefore not one-to-one, and both aciR functions are graded against this one
 extract.

Function: ref_aci_metric_scalar
Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Lines: 305-308
Inputs: filter_mean, filter_cov, smoother_mean, smoother_cov
Outputs: ACI_metric, signal_smoother_filter, cov_ratio_smoother_filter,
 dispersion_smoother_filter
Pairs: aci_metric
Note: The scalar ACI metric as a relative entropy of the smoother from the
 filter, split into its signal and dispersion parts. The parts are returned as
 well as the total so that a disagreement can be attributed to one of them
 rather than to their sum.

Function: ref_online_smoother_scalar
Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Lines: 326-431
Inputs: x, L_x, f_x, f_y, L_y, filter_mean, filter_cov, E_j_matrices,
 F_j_matrices, fixed_lag, N, dt
Outputs: online_fixed_mean, online_fixed_cov, update_matrices_fixed
Pairs: aci_online_smoother
Note: The fixed-lag online smoother, storing the full staggered triangle of
 posteriors as nested cell arrays. `fixed_lag` is an input because the
 reference sets it to N+1 one line above this range and never varies it; taking
 it as an argument is what allows the lag boundaries to be graded. The memory
 is quadratic in N -- at the published N = 30000 the three cell arrays come to
 roughly 10.8 GB, which is why the harness runs at reduced sizes.

Function: ref_cir_scalar
Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Lines: 433-546
Inputs: dt, T, time_start_plot, time_end_plot, online_fixed_mean,
 online_fixed_cov
Outputs: subjective_CIR, approx_objective_CIR, RE_metric, max_RE_metric,
 eps_ord_values, epsilon_resolution, lookahead_tolerance, first_idx, last_idx,
 plot_len
Pairs: aci_cir
Note: Subjective CIR over a grid of thresholds, and the computationally
 efficient objective CIR approximation. The epsilon grid is fixed inside this
 range at 513 logarithmically spaced points, where aciR defaults to 129, so the
 driver passes the reference grid explicitly. RE_metric is allocated
 plot_len-squared and reaches 3.40 GB at the published settings; aciR reduces
 each row on the spot and never forms it.

Function: ref_objective_cir_exact
Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Lines: 549-561
Inputs: eps_ord_values, subjective_CIR, max_RE_metric, lookahead_tolerance, dt
Outputs: defn_objective_CIR
Pairs: [absent in aciR]
Note: The objective CIR by its DEFINITION -- the subjective ranges integrated
 over the threshold grid and normalised by the peak divergence -- as distinct
 from the efficient underestimate computed inside ref_cir_scalar. aciR
 implements the underestimate and documents the definition, but does not
 compute this quantity. Recorded as a capability gap, not a divergence; see the
 findings section of the parity report.

Function: ref_dyad_setup_and_signals
Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Lines: 48-125
Inputs:
Outputs: x, y, L_x, f_x, f_y, L_y, Sx_1, Sx_2, Sy_1, Sy_2, N, dt, T, gamma,
 d_x, d_y, F_x, F_y, sigma_x, sigma_y
Pairs: aci_dyad_model, aci_dyad_components, aci_simulate
Note: Model setup and the Euler-Maruyama signal generation, including the
 reference's own rng(333). It takes no arguments and fixes N = 30000 inside the
 range, which is the point: it reproduces the PUBLISHED dataset exactly. That
 matters because the filter and smoother are O(N) and can therefore be graded
 at full scale even though the online smoother and CIR cannot. Its random draws
 are MATLAB's and are not reproducible in R, so it is never compared against
 aci_simulate() numerically -- only the components map is.

Function: ref_pp_filter_dir1
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 214-255
Inputs: x, y, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, Sx_1, Sx_2, Sy_1, Sy_2, N, dt
Outputs: filter_mean, filter_cov, S_yoS_y, S_yoS_y_inv, S_xoS_x, S_xoS_y,
 S_yoS_x
ConsumedOnly: x=1
Pairs: aci_filter
Note: Predator-prey, causal direction x(t) -> y. The PREY y is observed and the
 PREDATOR x is unobserved, so the roles of x and y are the mirror of the dyad's
 and the Grammian names read the other way round. The initial filter mean is
 the true unobserved value x(1), consumed at that index alone.

Function: ref_pp_smoother_dir1
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 320-384
Inputs: y, filter_mean, filter_cov, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, S_yoS_y_inv, S_xoS_x,
 S_xoS_y, S_yoS_x, N, dt
Outputs: smoother_mean, smoother_cov, E_j_matrices, F_j_matrices
Pairs: aci_smoother, .aci_online_aux
Note: Backward smoother and online-smoother auxiliaries for direction one, in
 one loop as in the dyad.

Function: ref_pp_metric_dir1
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 598-601
Inputs: filter_mean, filter_cov, smoother_mean, smoother_cov
Outputs: signal_smoother_filter, cov_ratio_smoother_filter,
 dispersion_smoother_filter, ACI_metric
Pairs: aci_metric
Note: ACI metric for direction one.

Function: ref_pp_filter_dir2
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 265-306
Inputs: x, y, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, Sx_1, Sx_2, Sy_1, Sy_2, N, dt
Outputs: filter_mean, filter_cov, S_xoS_x, S_xoS_x_inv, S_yoS_y, S_yoS_x,
 S_xoS_y
ConsumedOnly: y=1
Pairs: aci_filter
Note: Predator-prey, causal direction y(t) -> x. The PREDATOR x is observed and
 the PREY y unobserved. Same algebra as ref_pp_filter_dir1 with the labels
 exchanged -- which is precisely why the script's two directions collide: they
 assign the same names, so a top-to-bottom run leaves only this one standing.

Function: ref_pp_smoother_dir2
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 394-457
Inputs: x, filter_mean, filter_cov, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, S_xoS_x_inv, S_yoS_y,
 S_yoS_x, S_xoS_y, N, dt
Outputs: smoother_mean, smoother_cov, E_j_matrices, F_j_matrices
Pairs: aci_smoother, .aci_online_aux
Note: Backward smoother and online-smoother auxiliaries for direction two. One
 line shorter than its direction-one mirror, which is why the ranges are read
 off the file rather than derived by offset.

Function: ref_pp_metric_dir2
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 865-868
Inputs: filter_mean, filter_cov, smoother_mean, smoother_cov
Outputs: signal_smoother_filter, cov_ratio_smoother_filter,
 dispersion_smoother_filter, ACI_metric
Pairs: aci_metric
Note: ACI metric for direction two.

Function: ref_pp_setup_dir1
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 54-88, 93-114, 155-174
Inputs:
Outputs: x, y, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, Sx_1, Sx_2, Sy_1, Sy_2, N, dt, T, alpha,
 beta, gamma, delta, sigma_x, sigma_y
Pairs: aci_predprey_model, aci_predprey_components
Note: Common model setup, direction-one components, and direction-one signal
 generation, including the reference's own rng(42). Three ranges rather than
 one because the script interleaves its two causal directions; taking them in
 file order is exactly what a user does following its "RUN THIS CODE SECTION"
 instructions. N = 12000 is fixed inside the range, so this reproduces the
 PUBLISHED dataset -- which is what allows the committed fixtures to be
 re-graded at full scale, the filter and smoother both being O(N).

Function: ref_pp_setup_dir2
Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Lines: 54-88, 122-143, 182-201
Inputs:
Outputs: x, y, L_x, L_y, f_x, f_y, Sx_1, Sx_2, Sy_1, Sy_2, N, dt, T, alpha,
 beta, gamma, delta, sigma_x, sigma_y
Pairs: aci_predprey_model, aci_predprey_components
Note: The direction-two mirror. Shares the common setup range with
 ref_pp_setup_dir1, so both see the same rng(42) and the same realisation.
